  "version": "v1",
  "entries": {
    "con": {
      "word": "CON; CON-",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "- (cum, signifying with, together, etc. See Com-.",
          "partOfSpeech": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "Against the affirmative side; in opposition; on the negative side; -- The antithesis of pro, and usually in connection with it. See Pro.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adverb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To know; to understand; to acknowledge. [Obs.] Of muses, Hobbinol, I con no skill. Spenser. They say they con to heaven the highway. Spenser.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To study in order to know; to peruse; to learn; to commit to memory; to regard studiously. Fixedly did look Upon the muddy waters which he conned As if he had been reading in a book. Wodsworth. I did not come into Parliament to con my lesson. Burke. To con answer, to be able to answer. [Obs.] -- To con thanks, to thank; to acknowledge obligation. [Obs.] Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To conduct, or superintend the steering of (a vessel); to watch the course of (a vessel) and direct the helmsman how to steer.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
